NAME
    head - print the first lines (or bytes) of a file

SYNOPSIS
    head [-n N] [-c N] [FILE]
    head [-N] [FILE]
    head [OPTION]... -- [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the first N lines (default 10), or with -c the first N
    bytes, which is how a prefix of a binary file is taken. Without
    -c the whole file is read into a fixed buffer first, so only the
    first 128 lines (or 100 columns each) of any input are ever
    seen - a documented consequence of using a static buffer.

    A lone -- ends the options, and is how a file whose name begins
    with a dash is reached - `head -- -n5' reads the file called -n5,
    which without the separator is not reachable at all, since -n5 is
    how you ask for five lines.
